# Magento 2 Module Development Guidelines

Based on [Adobe Commerce PHP Development Guide](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/development/)

## Component Types

Adobe Commerce and Magento Open Source applications consist of three component types:

- **Modules** - Interact with other parts of the application to accomplish business functions or provide features. Can contain UI and application interfaces.
- **Themes** - Provide personalized look and feel for storefront or Admin (e.g., Blank theme, Luma theme).
- **Language Packages** - Assist in internationalization (i18n) and localization by providing translations.

## Technical Guidelines

Based on [Adobe Commerce Technical Guidelines](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/coding-standards/technical-guidelines/)

These guidelines are used as reference during code reviews and some rules have corresponding code checks in static tests. All new technical initiatives SHOULD follow these recommendations.

### Text Conventions

Use RFC2119 to interpret keywords:
- **MUST** and **MUST NOT** - Absolute requirements
- **SHOULD** and **SHOULD NOT** - Strong recommendations
- **MAY** - Optional/permissible
- **REQUIRED**, **RECOMMENDED**, **OPTIONAL**

### 1. Basic Programming Principles

#### 1.1 Strict Types
- All new PHP files **MUST** have strict type mode enabled: `declare(strict_types=1);`
- All updated PHP files **SHOULD** have strict type mode enabled
- PHP interfaces **MAY** have this declaration

#### 1.2 Return Types
- Explicit return types **MUST BE** declared on functions
- Type hints for scalar arguments **SHOULD** be used

#### 1.3 Function Arguments
- Function arguments **SHOULD NOT** be modified

**Example:**
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

class Example
{
    public function processData(array $data): array
    {
        // Process data without modifying input
        $result = [];
        foreach ($data as $item) {
            $result[] = $this->transform($item);
        }
        return $result;
    }
}
```

### 2. Class Design

#### 2.1 SOLID Principles
- Object decomposition **MUST** follow SOLID principles:
  - **S**ingle Responsibility Principle
  - **O**pen/Closed Principle
  - **L**iskov Substitution Principle
  - **I**nterface Segregation Principle
  - **D**ependency Inversion Principle

#### 2.2 Object Instantiation
- Factories **SHOULD** be used for object instantiation instead of `new` keyword
- Objects **SHOULD** be replaceable for testing or extensibility purposes
- Exception: DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) - no behavior, so no replaceability needed
- Examples of DTOs: Data interfaces, Exceptions, `Zend_Db_Expr`

**Not Recommended:**
```php
class OrderProcessor
{
    public function process()
    {
        $logger = new Logger(); // Direct instantiation
    }
}
```

**Recommended:**
```php
class OrderProcessor
{
    public function __construct(
        private LoggerFactory $loggerFactory
    ) {}

    public function process()
    {
        $logger = $this->loggerFactory->create();
    }
}
```

#### 2.3 Object Initialization
- An object **MUST** be ready for use after instantiation
- No additional public initialization methods (`init()`, `load()`) are allowed
- Use lazy loading within methods if needed

**Not Recommended:**
```php
class Config
{
    private $data;

    public function init()
    {
        $this->data = $this->fileReader->load('cache.xml');
    }

    public function getValue($key)
    {
        return $this->data[$key];
    }
}
```

**Recommended:**
```php
class Config
{
    private ?array $data = null;

    public function __construct(
        private FileReaderInterface $fileReader
    ) {}

    public function getValue(string $key): ?string
    {
        if ($this->data === null) {
            $this->data = $this->fileReader->load('cache.xml');
        }

        return $this->data[$key] ?? null;
    }
}
```

#### 2.4 Constructor Rules
- Constructor **SHOULD** only contain:
  - Dependency assignment operations
  - Argument validation operations
- Constructor **SHOULD** throw exceptions when validation fails
- Constructor **MUST NOT** trigger events
- No other operations are allowed

**Example:**
```php
class Composite
{
    private $renderers;

    /**
     * @param RendererInterface[] $renderers
     * @throws InvalidArgumentException
     */
    public function __construct(array $renderers)
    {
        foreach ($renderers as $renderer) {
            if (!$renderer instanceof RendererInterface) {
                throw new InvalidArgumentException(
                    sprintf('Instance of RendererInterface expected, got %s', get_class($renderer))
                );
            }
        }
        $this->renderers = $renderers;
    }
}
```

#### 2.5 Dependency Types
- All dependencies **MUST** be requested by the most generic type required
- Use interfaces, not concrete classes
- This enables polymorphism and testability

**Not Recommended:**
```php
class SessionManager
{
    public function __construct(RedisSessionAdapter $sessionAdapter)
    {}
}
```

**Recommended:**
```php
class SessionManager
{
    public function __construct(
        private SessionAdapterInterface $sessionAdapter
    ) {}
}
```

#### 2.6 Proxies and Interceptors
- Proxies and interceptors **MUST NEVER** be explicitly requested in constructors
- Let Magento's dependency injection handle proxy generation automatically

#### 2.7 Composition Over Inheritance
- Inheritance **SHOULD NOT** be used
- Composition **SHOULD** be used for code reuse
- Prefer object composition and dependency injection

**Not Recommended:**
```php
class AbstractController extends Action
{
    protected function validate($request) {}
    protected function generateHash($request) {}
}

class Edit extends AbstractController
{
    public function execute()
    {
        $this->validate($request);
    }
}
```

**Recommended:**
```php
class Edit extends Action
{
    public function __construct(
        Context $context,
        private ValidatorInterface $validator,
        private HashGeneratorInterface $hashGenerator
    ) {
        parent::__construct($context);
    }

    public function execute()
    {
        $this->validator->validate($request);
    }
}
```

### 3. Dependency Injection

#### 3.1 Constructor Injection
- **MUST** use constructor injection for all dependencies
- Configure dependencies in `etc/di.xml`
- Prefer interfaces over concrete classes
- Use virtual types for complex configurations

#### 3.2 Object Manager
- Object Manager **SHOULD NOT** be used directly in business logic
- Use Object Manager only in:
  - Factories
  - Proxies
  - Bootstrap code
  - Static code that cannot use DI

#### 3.3 Proxies
- Proxies provide lazy loading for expensive dependencies
- **MUST NEVER** request proxies explicitly in constructors
- Magento automatically generates proxies when needed

#### 3.4 Factories
- Factories **SHOULD** be used for object creation
- Factories are auto-generated by Magento
- Use factory pattern instead of `new` keyword

### 4. Interception (Plugins)

#### 4.1 Plugin Types
- **before** - Execute before target method
- **after** - Execute after target method
- **around** - Wrap target method execution (use sparingly)

#### 4.2 When to Use Plugins
- Use plugins to modify input/output of methods
- Use events for notifications and side effects
- Plugins **SHOULD** be used instead of events when modifying method behavior

#### 4.3 Plugin Guidelines
- Register plugins in `etc/di.xml`
- Use `sortOrder` to control execution order
- Avoid circular dependencies
- Limit use of `around` plugins (can increase stack traces and affect performance)

### 5. Exceptions

#### 5.1 Exception Handling
- **MUST** implement proper exception handling
- Constructor **SHOULD** throw exceptions on validation failure
- Use specific exception types, not generic `Exception`
- Log exceptions appropriately

**Example:**
```php
public function __construct(array $renderers)
{
    foreach ($renderers as $renderer) {
        if (!$renderer instanceof RendererInterface) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException(
                sprintf('Expected RendererInterface, got %s', get_class($renderer))
            );
        }
    }
    $this->renderers = $renderers;
}
```

### 6. Application Layers

#### 6.1 Presentation Layer
- Controllers handle HTTP requests/responses
- Blocks contain presentation logic
- Templates contain HTML/PHP markup
- **MUST** escape all output (see Output Escaping section for detailed guidelines)
- **MUST NOT** access data layer directly

#### 6.2 Data Access (Persistence) Layer
- Resource models handle database operations
- Collections handle data retrieval
- **MUST** use prepared statements for SQL queries
- **MUST NOT** contain business logic

#### 6.3 Service Contracts (Application) Layer
- Define interfaces in `Api/` directory
- Implement interfaces in `Model/` directory
- Use repositories for data access
- **MUST** use service contracts for public APIs

### 7. Configuration

#### 7.1 Configuration Management
- System configuration: `etc/adminhtml/system.xml`
- Default configuration: `etc/config.xml`
- Use configuration scopes (default, website, store)
- Sensitive settings: `etc/env.php` (excluded from version control)

### 8. Modularity

#### 8.1 Module Design
- Modules **SHOULD** be independent and loosely coupled
- Use service contracts for inter-module communication
- Avoid direct dependencies between modules when possible
- Follow single responsibility principle

### 9. Security

#### 9.1 SQL Injection Protection
- **MUST** use prepared statements for SQL queries
- **MUST NOT** concatenate user input into SQL queries
- Use resource models and collections for database access

**Not Recommended:**
```php
$connection->query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = " . $userId);
```

**Recommended:**
```php
$connection->select()->from('users')->where('id = ?', $userId);
```

#### 9.2 Authentication
- **SHOULD** implement multi-factor authentication where possible
- **MUST NOT** ship with default credentials
- **SHOULD** implement weak-password checks
- **SHOULD** align password policies with NIST 800-63 B guidelines
- **SHOULD** harden against account enumeration attacks
- **SHOULD** limit failed login attempts
- **MUST** use secure session management

#### 9.3 Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Protection
- **MUST** sanitize input
- **MUST** escape output
- **SHOULD** cast incoming data to expected type
- **SHOULD** check incoming string data length
- **SHOULD** drop special characters (null bytes) from input
- **MUST** use `$escaper` directly in templates (see Output Escaping section for detailed guidelines)

**Example:**
```php
<?php
/**
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */
?>
<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($userInput) ?>
<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($url) ?>
<?= $escaper->escapeJs($javascript) ?>
```

#### 9.4 Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) Protection
- **MUST** utilize CSRF token mechanism
- All data manipulation requests **MUST** use POST requests
- Validate CSRF tokens in controllers

#### 9.5 Access Control Lists (ACL)
- Modules introducing Admin Panel functionality **MUST** have ACL
- Define ACL resources in `etc/acl.xml`
- Check permissions in controllers

#### 9.6 Sensitive Data Exposure
- Exceptions/Notices/Warnings **MUST** be caught and logged
- Error output **MUST NOT** be displayed to users
- Logs **MUST NOT** contain sensitive information (credentials, etc.)
- Display standard messages to inform users

#### 9.7 Local File Inclusion (LFI) Protection
- User-submitted path and file names **MUST NOT** be trusted
- **SHOULD** sanitize paths to remove dot-dot-slash (`../`)
- Validate file paths against whitelist

#### 9.8 Remote Code Execution (RCE) Protection
- **MUST NOT** use: `eval()`, `passthru()`, `system()`, `shell_exec()`, `serialize()`, `unserialize()`, `md5()`, `srand()`, `mt_srand()`
- User-submitted values **MUST NOT** be passed to: `include*()`, `require*()`, `create_function()`, `fopen()`, `preg_replace()`
- Variable functions **MUST NOT** be used if variable values are user-submitted

#### 9.9 Security Implementation
- Security capabilities **SHOULD** be implemented at Framework level or in dedicated module(s)
- **MUST** be utilized centrally by entire application

#### 9.10 File Security
- Files **MUST** be secured by web server configuration (`.htaccess` or `nginx.conf`)
- Exception: Files intended to be publicly accessible

#### 9.11 Input Validation
- Presentation layer classes accessing user input **MUST NOT** assume it has been validated
- Always validate and sanitize user input

### 10. Events

#### 10.1 Event Observer Guidelines
- All values (including objects) passed to events **MUST NOT** be modified in observers
- Use plugins for modifying input/output of functions
- Events **SHOULD** be observed as specifically as possible
- **MUST NOT** use global subscription when area-specific is sufficient (e.g., frontend-only)
- Events **SHOULD NOT** change state of observable objects

**Not Recommended:**
```php
class Observer implements ObserverInterface
{
    public function execute(Observer $observer)
    {
        $transport = $observer->getData('transport');
        // Modifying transport violates rule - use plugin instead
        $transport->setData('modified', true);
    }
}
```

**Recommended:**
```php
class Plugin
{
    public function aroundExecute($subject, callable $proceed, $data)
    {
        // Modify input/output using plugin
        $modifiedData = $this->modifyData($data);
        return $proceed($modifiedData);
    }
}
```

### 11. Web API

#### 11.1 API Requirements
- Both REST and SOAP APIs **MUST** be exposed
- All Web API GET endpoints **MUST** return lists of entities
- Configure in `etc/webapi_rest/routes.xml` and `etc/webapi_soap/routes.xml`

### 12. Command Line Interface (CLI)

#### 12.1 CLI Guidelines
- CLI command naming guidelines **MUST** be followed
- CLI command **MUST** be created for functionality intended for system integrators/administrators/developers
- CLI command **MUST** always run in global area
- If command needs specific area, area **SHOULD** be set up before execution
- Exception in single CLI command **SHOULD NOT** break CLI framework

### 13. Cron

#### 13.1 Cron Guidelines
- Cron job **SHOULD** be an idempotent method
- Same cron job can be executed multiple times safely
- Handle duplicate execution gracefully

### 14. Testing

#### 14.1 White-Box Testing
- Test internal structure and implementation
- Unit tests: Test individual classes and methods
- Integration tests: Test module interactions
- Functional tests: Test end-to-end user flows

#### 14.2 Testing Patterns
- Use PHPUnit for unit and integration tests
- Use Magento Functional Testing Framework (MFTF) for functional tests
- Test all code paths
- Mock dependencies appropriately

### 15. Services

#### 15.1 Service Architecture
- New features with limited customization scenarios **SHOULD** be implemented as thin extension
- Extension communicates to service containing business logic
- Allows independent feature releases
- Makes feature upgrades easier

## Module Structure Requirements

### PSR-4 Compliance
- **MUST** follow PSR-4 compliant structure when building modules
- Module namespace must match directory structure
- Example: `HappyHorizon\Catalog` → `app/code/HappyHorizon/Catalog/`

### Required Files

#### 1. Registration File
- **Location**: `registration.php` (root of module directory)
- **Purpose**: Registers the module with Magento's component registrar
- **Format**:
```php
use Magento\Framework\Component\ComponentRegistrar;

ComponentRegistrar::register(
    ComponentRegistrar::MODULE,
    'VendorName_ModuleName',
    __DIR__
);
```

#### 2. Module Declaration
- **Location**: `etc/module.xml`
- **Purpose**: Declares module name and dependencies
- **Required Elements**:
  - Module name (must match registration)
  - Module sequence (dependencies)
- **Example**:
```xml
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Module/etc/module.xsd">
    <module name="VendorName_ModuleName">
        <sequence>
            <module name="Magento_Catalog"/>
        </sequence>
    </module>
</config>
```

#### 3. Module README
- **Location**: `README.md` (root of module directory)
- **Purpose**: Document the module for developers and maintainers
- **MUST** include:
  - **Description**: A short paragraph explaining what the module does: purpose, main functionality, how it fits in (e.g. “plugs into…”, “adds content type…”, “customises…”), and key dependencies where relevant. Go into enough detail that a maintainer can understand the module without reading the code first.
  - **Extension points**: An explicit inventory (do not use “See etc/di.xml” or “See etc/events.xml”; list each item):
    - **Plugins**: For each plugin, list the plugin class and the target class it intercepts (e.g. `Vendor\Module\Plugin\SomePlugin` on `Magento\Some\TargetClass`). Include area (frontend/adminhtml) when the plugin is area-specific. If the module has no plugins, state “None.”
    - **Observers**: For each observer, list the observer class and the event name (e.g. `Vendor\Module\Observer\SomeObserver` on event `event_name`). Include area when relevant. If the module has no observers, state “None.”
    - **Attributes**: List any EAV attributes, extension attributes, or custom attributes: entity (or interface) and attribute code (and type where useful, e.g. string, bool). If the module adds none, state “None.”
- **SHOULD** use a consistent structure with a “## Extension points” section and subsections for Plugins, Observers, and Attributes (each as a bullet list or short table).
- **SHOULD** be kept up to date when adding or removing plugins, observers, or attributes.

## Module File Structure

### Standard Directory Structure
```
app/code/VendorName/ModuleName/
├── registration.php              # Module registration (REQUIRED)
├── composer.json                 # Composer configuration (optional but recommended)
├── etc/
│   ├── module.xml                # Module declaration (REQUIRED)
│   ├── di.xml                    # Dependency injection configuration
│   ├── frontend/
│   │   └── routes.xml            # Frontend routes
│   ├── adminhtml/
│   │   └── routes.xml            # Admin routes
│   └── webapi_rest/
│       └── routes.xml            # REST API routes
├── Controller/                   # Controllers
│   ├── Index/
│   │   └── Index.php
│   └── Adminhtml/
│       └── Index/
│           └── Index.php
├── Block/                        # View blocks
├── Model/                        # Business logic models
├── Plugin/                       # Plugins (interceptors)
├── Observer/                     # Event observers
├── Api/                          # Service contracts (interfaces)
│   └── Data/
│       └── EntityInterface.php
├── view/
│   ├── frontend/
│   │   ├── layout/               # Frontend layouts
│   │   └── templates/            # Frontend templates (.phtml)
│   └── adminhtml/
│       ├── layout/               # Admin layouts
│       └── templates/            # Admin templates (.phtml)
├── i18n/                         # Translation files
│   └── en_US.csv
└── README.md                     # Module documentation
```

## Module Development Best Practices

### Naming Conventions
- **Vendor Name**: Use company/organization name (e.g., `HappyHorizon`)
- **Module Name**: Use descriptive, PascalCase name (e.g., ``Catalog`, `Checkout`)
- **Full Module Name**: `VendorName_ModuleName` (e.g., `HappyHorizon_Catalog`)

### Code Organization
- Group related functionality by component
- Use dependency injection for all dependencies
- Follow Magento coding standards ([Magento PHP Coding Standards](https://developer.adobe.com/commerce/php/coding-standards/php/))
- Use service contracts (interfaces) for public APIs
- Implement proper exception handling
- **SHOULD NOT** use inheritance - prefer composition over inheritance
- **SHOULD NOT** create helper classes with static methods - use proper dependency injection instead
- Objects **MUST** be ready for use after instantiation (no `init()` or `load()` methods)
- Factories **SHOULD** be used for object creation instead of `new` keyword (except DTOs)
- Constructor **SHOULD** only contain dependency assignment and argument validation
- Constructor **MUST NOT** trigger events or perform other operations

### Controllers
- **Frontend Controllers**: Implement `HttpGetActionInterface` or `HttpPostActionInterface`
- **Admin Controllers**: Extend `Magento\Backend\App\Action` and implement `HttpGetActionInterface`
- Use proper authorization checks (`ADMIN_RESOURCE` constant for admin)
- Return proper result types (`ResultInterface`)

### Plugins (Interceptors)
- Use plugins to modify behavior of existing methods
- Three types: `before`, `after`, `around`
- Register plugins in `etc/di.xml`
- Use `sortOrder` to control plugin execution order
- Avoid circular dependencies
- Limit use of `around` plugins (can increase stack traces and affect performance)
- Use plugins for modifying input/output; use events for notifications and side effects

### Dependency Injection
- Configure in `etc/di.xml`
- **MUST** use constructor injection for all dependencies
- Prefer interfaces over concrete classes (request most generic type)
- Use virtual types for complex configurations
- **MUST NEVER** request proxies or interceptors explicitly in constructors
- Factories **SHOULD** be used instead of `new` keyword (except DTOs)
- Object Manager **SHOULD NOT** be used directly in business logic
- Proxies provide lazy loading and are auto-generated by Magento when needed

### Routing
- **Frontend Routes**: `etc/frontend/routes.xml` with router `id="standard"`
- **Admin Routes**: `etc/adminhtml/routes.xml` with router `id="admin"`
- **API Routes**: `etc/webapi_rest/routes.xml` for REST APIs
- Route format: `frontName/controller/action`

### Views and Layouts
- **Layout XML**: Define page structure and blocks
- **Templates**: Use `.phtml` files for presentation logic
- **Blocks**: Separate business logic from presentation
- **MUST** use proper escaping (see Output Escaping section below)
- **MUST** use `$escaper` directly in templates (not deprecated block methods)

## Output Escaping

### Escaper in Templates
- **MUST** escape all output in templates to prevent XSS attacks
- The `$escaper` variable is automatically available in all `.phtml` templates
- **MUST** use `$escaper` directly (not deprecated block methods)
- **MUST** declare `$escaper` in template DocBlock for clarity

### Escaping Methods

#### escapeHtml()
- **Use for**: HTML content (text nodes, innerHTML)
- **Escapes**: `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'`
- **Example**: `<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($block->getTitle()) ?>`
- **Optional parameter**: `$allowedTags` - array of allowed HTML tags

#### escapeHtmlAttr()
- **Use for**: HTML attribute values
- **Escapes**: All HTML special characters including quotes
- **Example**: `<div class="<?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr($block->getCssClass()) ?>">`
- **Optional parameter**: `$escapeSingleQuote` (default: true)

#### escapeJs()
- **Use for**: JavaScript strings and code
- **Escapes**: JavaScript special characters, quotes, newlines
- **Example**: `var name = '<?= $escaper->escapeJs($block->getName()) ?>';`

#### escapeJsQuote()
- **Use for**: JavaScript strings with specific quote character
- **Escapes**: JavaScript special characters for quoted strings
- **Example**: `var data = '<?= $escaper->escapeJsQuote($block->getData(), "'") ?>';`
- **Parameters**: `$data`, `$quote` (default: `'\''`)

#### escapeUrl()
- **Use for**: URL values in href attributes
- **Escapes**: URL-unsafe characters
- **Example**: `<a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getUrl()) ?>">`

#### escapeCss()
- **Use for**: CSS values
- **Escapes**: CSS special characters
- **Example**: `style="color: <?= $escaper->escapeCss($block->getColor()) ?>;"`

### Template Example
```php
<?php
/**
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */
?>
<h1><?= $escaper->escapeHtml($block->getTitle()) ?></h1>
<a href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($block->getUrl()) ?>"
   class="<?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr($block->getCssClass()) ?>">
    <?= $escaper->escapeHtml($block->getLinkText()) ?>
</a>
<script>
    var productName = '<?= $escaper->escapeJs($block->getProductName()) ?>';
</script>
```

### Best Practices
- **MUST** escape all user-generated or database-sourced content
- **MUST** escape content even if it's "trusted" - defense in depth
- **MUST NOT** double-escape (don't escape already-escaped content)
- **MUST** use the appropriate escaping method for the context (HTML vs JS vs URL)
- **SHOULD** use `escapeHtml()` for most text content
- **SHOULD** use `escapeHtmlAttr()` for all HTML attribute values
- **SHOULD** use `escapeJs()` for all JavaScript string values
- **MUST NOT** use deprecated block methods (`$block->escapeHtml()`, etc.) - use `$escaper` directly

## Module Lifecycle

### Enable/Disable
```bash
# Enable module
php bin/magento module:enable VendorName_ModuleName

# Disable module
php bin/magento module:disable VendorName_ModuleName

# Run setup upgrade after enabling
php bin/magento setup:upgrade

# Clear cache
php bin/magento cache:flush
```

### Module Status
- Check module status: `php bin/magento module:status`
- Modules can be enabled/disabled without code changes
- Disabled modules are not loaded by the application

## Common Patterns

### Service Contracts
- Define interfaces in `Api/` directory
- Implement interfaces in `Model/` directory
- Use repositories for data access
- Prefer service contracts over direct model access

### Events and Observers
- Declare observers in `etc/events.xml` or `etc/frontend/events.xml`
- Observer classes in `Observer/` directory
- Use events for loose coupling between modules

### Configuration
- System configuration: `etc/adminhtml/system.xml`
- Default configuration: `etc/config.xml`
- Use configuration scopes (default, website, store)

### Database Schema
- Use declarative schema: `etc/db_schema.xml`
- Migration scripts: `Setup/Patch/Data/` and `Setup/Patch/Schema/`
- Avoid direct SQL queries; use resource models

## Testing

### White-Box Testing
- Test internal structure and implementation
- Cover all code paths
- Mock dependencies appropriately

### Unit Tests
- Location: `Test/Unit/`
- Use PHPUnit
- Test individual classes and methods
- Test in isolation with mocked dependencies

### Integration Tests
- Location: `Test/Integration/`
- Test module interactions
- Use Magento test framework
- Test with real database and dependencies

### Functional Tests
- Use Magento Functional Testing Framework (MFTF)
- Test end-to-end user flows
- Test from user perspective
- Use test data fixtures

### Testing Best Practices
- Write tests for all new code
- Test both success and failure scenarios
- Use data providers for multiple test cases
- Follow AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)

## Deprecated Functions and Methods

### General Rules
- **MUST NOT** use deprecated functions, methods, or classes
- **MUST** check PHPDoc `@deprecated` tags before using any method
- **MUST** use recommended alternatives when available
- **MUST** update existing code that uses deprecated functionality
- Deprecated code may be removed in future Magento versions
- Static analysis tools **SHOULD** be configured to detect deprecated usage

### Common Deprecated Patterns in Modules

#### Block Escaping Methods (Deprecated since 103.0.0)
- **MUST NOT** use: `$block->escapeHtml()`, `$block->escapeJs()`, `$block->escapeHtmlAttr()`, `$block->escapeUrl()`, `$block->escapeCss()`
- **MUST** use: `$escaper->escapeHtml()`, `$escaper->escapeJs()`, `$escaper->escapeHtmlAttr()`, `$escaper->escapeUrl()`, `$escaper->escapeCss()`
- See Output Escaping section above for detailed escaping guidelines

**Deprecated:**
```php
<?= $block->escapeHtml($data) ?>
<?= $block->escapeJs($data) ?>
<?= $block->escapeUrl($url) ?>
```

**Correct:**
```php
<?php
/**
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */
?>
<?= $escaper->escapeHtml($data) ?>
<?= $escaper->escapeJs($data) ?>
<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($url) ?>
```

#### Other Deprecated Block Methods (Deprecated since 101.0.0)
- **MUST NOT** use: `$block->escapeXssInUrl()`, `$block->escapeQuote()`, `$block->escapeJsQuote()`
- **MUST** use: `$escaper->escapeXssInUrl()`, `$escaper->escapeQuote()`, `$escaper->escapeJsQuote()`

#### Object Manager Direct Usage
- **MUST NOT** use `ObjectManager::getInstance()` directly in business logic
- **MUST** use dependency injection instead
- Object Manager **SHOULD** only be used in factories, proxies, bootstrap code, or static code

**Deprecated:**
```php
$objectManager = \Magento\Framework\App\ObjectManager::getInstance();
$product = $objectManager->get(\Magento\Catalog\Model\Product::class);
```

**Correct:**
```php
use Magento\Catalog\Model\ProductFactory;

class MyClass
{
    public function __construct(
        private ProductFactory $productFactory
    ) {}

    public function doSomething()
    {
        $product = $this->productFactory->create();
    }
}
```

#### Direct Model Instantiation
- **MUST NOT** use `new` keyword for Magento models
- **MUST** use factories instead
- Exception: DTOs (Data Transfer Objects) may use `new`

**Deprecated:**
```php
$product = new \Magento\Catalog\Model\Product();
```

**Correct:**
```php
use Magento\Catalog\Model\ProductFactory;

public function __construct(
    private ProductFactory $productFactory
) {}

public function createProduct()
{
    $product = $this->productFactory->create();
}
```

#### DataObject Magic Methods
- **MUST NOT** use `__get()`, `__set()`, `__isset()`, `__unset()` on `\Magento\Framework\DataObject`
- **MUST** use explicit getter/setter methods or `getData()`, `setData()`

**Deprecated:**
```php
$object = new \Magento\Framework\DataObject();
$object->name = 'value'; // Magic setter
$value = $object->name;  // Magic getter
```

**Correct:**
```php
$object = new \Magento\Framework\DataObject();
$object->setData('name', 'value');
$value = $object->getData('name');
```

### Checking for Deprecations
- **SHOULD** run static tests: `vendor/bin/phpcs --standard=vendor/magento/magento-coding-standard/Magento app/code/`
- **SHOULD** configure PHPStan to detect deprecated method calls
- **SHOULD** check IDE warnings for deprecated method usage
- **SHOULD** review Magento release notes for deprecation announcements
- **SHOULD** use `grep -r "@deprecated" vendor/magento/` to find deprecated methods

### Migration Strategy
1. Identify all deprecated method calls in module
2. Replace with recommended alternatives
3. Update unit and integration tests
4. Test thoroughly after migration
5. Update code reviews to catch deprecated usage
6. Document migration in module changelog

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] No deprecated methods used
- [ ] All escaping uses `$escaper` directly
- [ ] No direct Object Manager usage
- [ ] No direct model instantiation with `new`
- [ ] No magic methods on DataObject
- [ ] Static analysis passes without deprecation warnings

## Web APIs

### API Requirements
- Both REST and SOAP APIs **MUST** be exposed for the same functionality
- All Web API GET endpoints **MUST** return lists of entities
- Configure REST API routes in `etc/webapi_rest/routes.xml`
- Configure SOAP API routes in `etc/webapi_soap/routes.xml`
- Use service contracts (interfaces) for API endpoints
- Implement proper authentication and authorization
- When the module exposes a Web API (e.g. `etc/webapi_rest/routes.xml` or `etc/webapi.xml` is present), the module **MUST** include API documentation with example calls (e.g. in the module README or a dedicated `docs/api.md`), covering each endpoint with sample request/response and authentication where applicable

### REST API
- Use RESTful conventions
- Return proper HTTP status codes
- Use JSON for request/response
- Document with Swagger/OpenAPI

### SOAP API
- Define WSDL in `etc/webapi_soap/routes.xml`
- Use SOAP standards
- Support both SOAP 1.1 and SOAP 1.2

### GraphQL
- Consider GraphQL for flexible queries
- Define schema in `etc/schema.graphqls`
- Implement resolvers for data fetching

## Component Development Lifecycle

### Prepare Phase
- Plan component structure
- Identify dependencies
- Design service contracts
- Plan database schema

### Build Phase
- Create module structure
- Implement functionality
- Write tests
- Follow coding standards

### Package Phase
- Create composer.json
- Package for distribution
- Create documentation
- Version appropriately

### Validate Phase
- Run static tests (PHPCS, PHPStan)
- Run unit and integration tests
- Test in different environments
- Validate against Magento standards

### Component Load Order
- Modules load in dependency order
- Define dependencies in `etc/module.xml` `<sequence>`
- Magento resolves load order automatically
- Circular dependencies are not allowed

## Composer Integration

### composer.json Structure
```json
{
    "name": "vendor/module-name",
    "description": "Module description",
    "type": "magento2-module",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "license": "proprietary",
    "require": {
        "php": "~8.1.0||~8.2.0||~8.3.0",
        "magento/framework": "*",
        "magento/module-catalog": "*"
    },
    "autoload": {
        "files": [
            "registration.php"
        ],
        "psr-4": {
            "Vendor\\ModuleName\\": ""
        }
    }
}
```

### Autoloading
- Use PSR-4 autoloading
- Register module in `registration.php`
- Composer handles autoloading automatically
- Run `composer dump-autoload` after changes

## Advanced Patterns

### EAV and Extension Attributes
- EAV (Entity-Attribute-Value) for flexible attributes
- Extension attributes for custom data on entities
- Define extension attributes in `etc/extension_attributes.xml`
- Use repositories to load entities with extension attributes

**Example:**
```xml
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Api/etc/extension_attributes.xsd">
    <extension_attributes for="Magento\Catalog\Api\Data\ProductInterface">
        <attribute code="custom_attribute" type="string"/>
    </extension_attributes>
</config>
```

### Repository Pattern
- Define repository interface in `Api/` directory
- Implement repository in `Model/` directory
- Use repositories for all data access
- Repositories return service contract interfaces

**Example:**
```php
// Api/EntityRepositoryInterface.php
interface EntityRepositoryInterface
{
    public function getById(int $id): EntityInterface;
    public function save(EntityInterface $entity): EntityInterface;
    public function delete(EntityInterface $entity): bool;
}
```

### Search Repositories
- Use search criteria for filtering
- Implement `SearchCriteriaInterface` for queries
- Return `SearchResultsInterface` with results
- Support pagination, sorting, filtering

## Database and Schema

### Declarative Schema
- Use `etc/db_schema.xml` for schema definition
- Magento automatically creates/updates tables
- Supports tables, columns, indexes, constraints
- Version-controlled schema changes

**Example:**
```xml
<schema xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Setup/Declaration/Schema/etc/schema.xsd">
    <table name="custom_table" resource="default" engine="innodb" comment="Custom Table">
        <column xsi:type="int" name="entity_id" unsigned="true" nullable="false" identity="true" comment="Entity ID"/>
        <column xsi:type="varchar" name="name" length="255" nullable="false" comment="Name"/>
        <constraint xsi:type="primary" referenceId="PRIMARY">
            <column name="entity_id"/>
        </constraint>
    </table>
</schema>
```

### Data Patches
- Location: `Setup/Patch/Data/`
- Use for data migrations
- Implement `DataPatchInterface`
- Run once per installation/upgrade

**Example:**
```php
class AddDefaultData implements DataPatchInterface
{
    public function apply()
    {
        // Add default data
        return $this;
    }

    public static function getDependencies()
    {
        return [];
    }

    public function getAliases()
    {
        return [];
    }
}
```

### Schema Patches
- Location: `Setup/Patch/Schema/`
- Use for schema changes not covered by declarative schema
- Implement `SchemaPatchInterface`
- Use sparingly - prefer declarative schema

## Message Queues

### Asynchronous Operations
- Use message queues for long-running operations
- Improve user experience with background processing
- Configure queues in `etc/queue_topology.xml` and `etc/queue_publisher.xml`

### Queue Configuration
```xml
<!-- etc/queue_topology.xml -->
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework-message-queue:etc/topology.xsd">
    <exchange name="custom.exchange" type="topic" connection="amqp">
        <binding id="custom.binding" topic="custom.topic" destinationType="queue" destination="custom.queue"/>
    </exchange>
</config>
```

### Topics
- Define topics in `etc/communication.xml`
- Topics represent message types
- Consumers process messages from topics

### Bulk Operations
- Use bulk APIs for multiple operations
- More efficient than individual API calls
- Implement `BulkOperationInterface`
- Process in background via message queues

## Caching

### Partial Caching
- Cache specific blocks or data
- Use cache tags for invalidation
- Implement `IdentityInterface` in blocks
- Cache lifetime configuration

### Full Page Caching
- Cache entire pages for anonymous users
- Use Varnish or built-in FPC
- Configure in `etc/cache.xml`
- Cache invalidation via tags

### Custom Cache Types
- Define custom cache types in `etc/cache.xml`
- Use for module-specific caching
- Configure cache backend (file, Redis, etc.)

**Example:**
```xml
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Cache/etc/cache.xsd">
    <type name="custom_cache" translate="label,description" instance="Vendor\Module\Model\Cache\Type">
        <label>Custom Cache</label>
        <description>Custom cache type description</description>
    </type>
</config>
```

### Cache Tags and Invalidation
- Use cache tags to group related cache entries
- Invalidate by tag when data changes
- Tags defined in `etc/cache.xml`
- Automatic invalidation on entity save/delete

## Advanced Development Topics

### Indexing and Indexers
- Indexers improve query performance
- Define indexers in `etc/indexer.xml`
- Implement `IndexerInterface`
- Support full and partial reindexing

**Example:**
```xml
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Indexer/etc/indexer.xsd">
    <indexer id="custom_indexer" view_id="custom_indexer" class="Vendor\Module\Model\Indexer\Custom">
        <title translate="true">Custom Indexer</title>
        <description translate="true">Indexes custom data</description>
    </indexer>
</config>
```

### Custom Indexer Creation
- Create indexer class implementing `IndexerInterface`
- Define index structure
- Implement `executeFull()` and `executeRow()`
- Register in `etc/indexer.xml`

### View Models
- Use view models for complex presentation logic
- Better than putting logic in blocks
- Implement `ViewModelInterface`
- Inject into templates via layout XML

**Example:**
```xml
<block class="Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template" name="custom.block">
    <arguments>
        <argument name="view_model" xsi:type="object">Vendor\Module\ViewModel\Custom</argument>
    </arguments>
</block>
```

### Adapters Pattern
- Use adapters to wrap third-party libraries
- Create adapter interface
- Implement adapter for library
- Allows easy library replacement

### Variable Pool
- Share data between layout elements
- Use `$block->getData('key')` and `$block->setData('key', $value)`
- Useful for passing data to child blocks
- Scope limited to layout rendering

